{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 16, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Crusader nobleman and one of the most prominent rulers of the County of Tripoli during the Middle Ages. Born around 1140 he was the second son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierne of Milly. Raymond III served as the regent for his nephew after Raymond II’s death in 1152 but later became count in his own right possibly through rebellion or succession disputes.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n\n**Early life and regency**\nRaymond III’s early life was marked by political maneuvering. After his father Raymond II died in 1152 Raymond III initially acted as regent for his nephew Bohemond III who was still a minor. However tensions arose between Raymond III and Bohemond’s mother who also sought influence. By 1153 Raymond III had secured control over Tripoli with Bohemond III’s acquiescence though the legitimacy of his rule remained contested. Raymond III’s reign saw efforts to stabilize the county’s borders and consolidate power against rival Muslim forces and neighboring Crusader states.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\n**Military campaigns and conflicts**\nRaymond III faced numerous military challenges particularly against the Muslim forces of Nur ad-Din and later Saladin. In 1164 he joined a Crusader alliance led by Amalric I of Jerusalem to resist Nur ad-Din’s advances. While initially successful the alliance fragmented and Raymond III’s troops suffered defeats. His most notable engagement came during the Siege of Harim in 1167 where he fought alongside Byzantine forces. Despite some early successes the Crusaders ultimately failed to capture Harim and Raymond III’s reputation took a hit. His later years saw continued conflict with Saladin who had become the dominant Muslim leader in the region.\n\n**Marriage and alliances**\nRaymond III’s political strategy heavily relied on strategic marriages. His first marriage was to a member of the Lusignan family though details of his first wife remain scarce. His second marriage to Eschiva of Bures in 1176 cemented important alliances with the powerful Bures family of Syria. This union produced several children including Raymond IV who would become the next Count of Tripoli. Eschiva’s connections were crucial in maintaining Raymond III’s power and securing his legacy. His marriages also reflected the broader Crusader practice of forming alliances through intermarriage to strengthen political positions.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n\n**Later years and death**\nRaymond III’s later years were characterized by increasing isolation and declining health. By the 1180s Saladin’s victories over the Crusader states left Tripoli under constant pressure. Raymond III’s attempts to form new alliances with the Byzantine Empire and other Crusader leaders proved largely ineffective. He died around 1187 though the exact circumstances of his death remain unclear. His reign marked a period of transition for the County of Tripoli shifting from a regional powerhouse to a beleaguered state on the fringes of the Crusader domains. Raymond III’s legacy endured through his son Raymond IV who continued the struggle to preserve Tripoli’s independence against the rising power of Saladin.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.08366527408361435}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.0001662831346038729}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.005333361681550741}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.24919773638248444}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00035089056473225355}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -0.01817992888391018}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.4510277509689331}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.09894715994596481}, {"token": " Crus", "logprob": -1.333800196647644}, {"token": "ader", "logprob": -9.250213042832911e-05}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.6472116112709045}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.08105096966028214}, {"token": " and", "logp